.TH XF 1 "January 2026" "xf" "User Commands"
.\"
.\" ===== NAME =====
.\"
.SH NAME
xf \- intent-driven terminal text editor
.\"
.\" ===== SYNOPSIS =====
.\"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xf
.RI [ options ]
.IR file " ..."
.br
.B xf
.B \-\-batch
.I script
.IR file " ..."
.br
.B xf
.B \-\-replay
.I script
.IR file " ..."
.\"
.\" ===== DESCRIPTION =====
.\"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xf
is a terminal-based text editor that takes a radically
different approach to editing: instead of navigating with a cursor, you
.B declare your intent
by describing
.I what
text you want to modify and
.I what
you want to do with it.
.PP
Unlike traditional editors that rely on cursor movement and modal editing,
.B xf
is built on these core principles:
.TP
.B No cursor
There is no concept of "current position" or cursor movement. All operations
act on explicit selections derived from declarative queries.
.TP
.B Selection-first editing
Users first describe the text they intend to operate on (e.g., "lines matching
TODO", "functions containing malloc"). Selections are named, composable, and
refinable.
.TP
.B Transactional editing
No command mutates text immediately. Each operation computes a selection,
applies a transformation, and stages the changes. Changes are only written to
disk when explicitly committed.
.TP
.B Diff-centric feedback
The primary visual output is a unified diff showing proposed changes. Users
evaluate edits by their effects, not by cursor position.
.TP
.B Reproducibility
Command history can be saved and replayed. Editing sessions are deterministic
and scriptable.
.PP
.B xf
is not a Vim or Emacs alternative. It is a different interaction model
optimized for correctness, reproducibility, and large-scale transformations.
.\"
.\" ===== OPTIONS =====
.\"
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-batch " script"
Execute commands from
.I script
and exit without entering interactive mode. This is useful for automated
refactoring and scripting. Each command in the script is executed in sequence,
and
.B xf
exits after the last command.
.TP
.BI \-\-replay " script"
Execute commands from
.I script
and then enter interactive mode. This is useful for setting up an initial
state before manual editing.
.TP
.I file ...
One or more files to edit. Multiple files are loaded into a unified buffer,
and commands operate across all files simultaneously. On commit, changes are
written back to each original file.
.\"
.\" ===== COMMANDS =====
.\"
.SH COMMANDS
.B xf
uses a declarative command language entered at an interactive prompt. Commands
describe intent rather than keystrokes. All commands are case-sensitive.
.\"
.\" ----- Selection Commands -----
.\"
.SS Selection Commands
Selection commands define the text that subsequent transformation commands
will operate on. Selections can be named for later reference.
.TP
.B select lines
Select all lines in the buffer.
.TP
.BI "select lines matching """ pattern """"
Select lines containing the literal substring
.IR pattern .
.TP
.BI "select lines matching /" regex /
Select lines matching the POSIX Extended Regular Expression
.IR regex .
.TP
.B select paragraphs
Select all paragraph blocks. A paragraph is a contiguous group of non-empty
lines separated by one or more blank lines.
.TP
.BI "select paragraphs containing """ pattern """"
Select only paragraphs that contain the substring
.IR pattern .
.TP
.B select blocks
Select indentation-based blocks. A block is a contiguous group of lines that
starts at column 0 (no leading whitespace) and includes all indented lines
that follow.
.TP
.BI "select blocks containing """ pattern """"
Select only blocks that contain the substring
.IR pattern .
.TP
.B select functions
Select C-style function definitions using heuristic detection. This identifies
function definitions based on common C syntax patterns.
.TP
.BI "select functions containing """ pattern """"
Select only functions that contain the substring
.IR pattern .
.TP
.BI "select ... as " name
Any select command can end with
.BI "as " name
to save the selection with a name for later reference. Named selections can
be viewed with the
.B selections
command.
.\"
.\" ----- Refinement Commands -----
.\"
.SS Refinement Commands
Refinement commands narrow down an existing selection by applying additional
filters. They operate on the current selection and produce a new, smaller
selection.
.TP
.BI "refine containing """ pattern """"
Keep only ranges that contain the substring
.IR pattern .
Ranges that do not contain the pattern are removed from the selection.
.TP
.BI "refine containing /" regex /
Keep only ranges that match the POSIX Extended Regular Expression
.IR regex .
.TP
.BI "refine excluding """ pattern """"
Remove ranges that contain the substring
.IR pattern .
Only ranges without the pattern remain in the selection.
.TP
.BI "refine excluding /" regex /
Remove ranges that match the POSIX Extended Regular Expression
.IR regex .
.TP
.B refine excluding comments
Remove ranges that consist entirely of comments. This uses heuristic detection
of C-style comments (both
.B //
line comments and
.B /* */
block comments).
.\"
.\" ----- Transformation Commands -----
.\"
.SS Transformation Commands
Transformation commands modify the selected text. All transformations are
.B staged
and must be
.B committed
to take effect. Use
.B preview
to see staged changes before committing.
.TP
.BI "replace """ old """ with """ new """"
Replace all occurrences of substring
.I old
with
.I new
within the selected lines. Both single and double quotes are supported.
.TP
.B delete
Delete all selected lines. The lines are removed from the buffer.
.TP
.BI "insert """ text """ before"
Insert a line containing
.I text
before each selected range.
.TP
.BI "insert """ text """ after"
Insert a line containing
.I text
after each selected range.
.TP
.BI "move before " line
Move all selected lines to appear before line number
.IR line .
.TP
.BI "move after " line
Move all selected lines to appear after line number
.IR line .
.\"
.\" ----- Preview and Commit Commands -----
.\"
.SS Preview and Commit Commands
These commands control the staging and application of changes.
.TP
.B preview
Display a unified diff of all staged changes. The diff shows exactly what
will change when
.B commit
is executed. If there are no staged changes, a message is displayed.
.TP
.B commit
Apply all staged changes to disk. Each modified file is written with the
updated content. After committing, the staged changes become the new baseline.
.TP
.B abort
Discard all staged changes without writing to disk. This also clears the
current selection.
.\"
.\" ----- History and Replay Commands -----
.\"
.SS History and Replay Commands
These commands manage command history for reproducibility and scripting.
.TP
.B history
Display all commands executed in the current session, numbered sequentially.
.TP
.BI "save " file
Save the command history to
.IR file .
The saved file can be used with
.B \-\-batch
or
.B \-\-replay
options, or with the
.B replay
command.
.TP
.BI "replay " file
Execute commands from
.IR file .
Each line in the file is executed as a command. Lines starting with
.B #
are treated as comments and ignored.
.\"
.\" ----- Utility Commands -----
.\"
.SS Utility Commands
.TP
.B selections
List all named selections with their range and line counts.
.TP
.BR help ", " ?
Display a summary of available commands.
.TP
.BR quit ", " q
Exit
.BR xf .
.B Warning:
.B xf
does not warn about uncommitted changes. Use
.B preview
before quitting to verify you have committed all desired changes.
.\"
.\" ===== PATTERNS =====
.\"
.SH PATTERNS
.B xf
supports two types of patterns for matching text:
.TP
.BI """" pattern """"
.B Substring matching.
Matches any line or range containing the literal string
.IR pattern .
Both single and double quotes are supported. This is faster but less flexible
than regex matching.
.TP
.BI / regex /
.B Regular expression matching.
Matches using POSIX Extended Regular Expressions (ERE). Common regex features
include:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B .
\- Match any single character
.IP \(bu 2
.B *
\- Match zero or more of the preceding element
.IP \(bu 2
.B +
\- Match one or more of the preceding element
.IP \(bu 2
.B ?
\- Match zero or one of the preceding element
.IP \(bu 2
.B ^
\- Match start of line
.IP \(bu 2
.B $
\- Match end of line
.IP \(bu 2
.B [abc]
\- Match any character in the set
.IP \(bu 2
.B [^abc]
\- Match any character not in the set
.IP \(bu 2
.B (a|b)
\- Match either a or b
.IP \(bu 2
.B [[:space:]]
\- Match whitespace (POSIX character class)
.IP \(bu 2
.B [[:alpha:]]
\- Match alphabetic characters
.IP \(bu 2
.B [[:digit:]]
\- Match digits
.RE
.\"
.\" ===== SCRIPT FILES =====
.\"
.SH SCRIPT FILES
Script files are plain text files containing one command per line. They can
be used with the
.BR \-\-batch ,
.BR \-\-replay ,
and
.B replay
commands.
.PP
Lines starting with
.B #
are treated as comments and ignored. Empty lines are also ignored.
.PP
Example script file
.RB ( refactor.xf ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
# Refactor script: replace malloc with xmalloc
# in all functions that use memory allocation

select functions containing "malloc"
replace "malloc" with "xmalloc"
commit

# Remove debug statements
select lines matching "DEBUG"
delete
commit
.fi
.RE
.\"
.\" ===== MULTI-FILE EDITING =====
.\"
.SH MULTI-FILE EDITING
When multiple files are specified on the command line,
.B xf
loads them into a unified buffer. Each file's content is concatenated, with
.B xf
tracking the original file boundaries.
.PP
Commands operate across all loaded files simultaneously. For example,
.B select lines matching "TODO"
will find matching lines in all files.
.PP
On
.BR commit ,
changes are written back to each original file, preserving the file boundaries.
The commit output shows each file separately:
.PP
.RS
.nf
> commit
xf: committed 200 lines to src/main.c
xf: committed 150 lines to src/utils.c
xf: committed 300 lines to src/parser.c
.fi
.RE
.\"
.\" ===== WORKFLOW =====
.\"
.SH WORKFLOW
A typical
.B xf
editing session follows this pattern:
.IP 1. 4
.B Select
the text you want to work with using a selection command.
.IP 2. 4
.B Refine
the selection if needed to narrow it down further.
.IP 3. 4
.B Transform
the selected text using replace, delete, insert, or move.
.IP 4. 4
.B Preview
the staged changes to verify they are correct.
.IP 5. 4
.B Commit
to apply the changes to disk, or
.B abort
to discard them.
.IP 6. 4
Repeat as needed, then
.B quit
to exit.
.PP
Multiple transformations can be staged before a single commit. The
.B preview
command shows all staged changes as a combined diff.
.\"
.\" ===== EXAMPLES =====
.\"
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic Session
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ xf myfile.c
xf: loaded myfile.c (156 lines)

> select lines matching "TODO"
xf: 5 range(s), 5 line(s) selected

> replace "TODO" with "DONE"
xf: 5 replacement(s) staged

> preview
--- original
+++ modified
@@ -10,1 +10,1 @@
-// TODO: implement error handling
+// DONE: implement error handling
...

> commit
xf: committed 156 lines to myfile.c

> quit
.fi
.RE
.SS Batch Mode
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ xf --batch refactor.xf src/*.c
.fi
.RE
.SS Regex Patterns
.PP
.RS
.nf
> select lines matching /TODO|FIXME/
> select lines matching /^[[:space:]]+if[[:space:]]*\\(/
.fi
.RE
.PP
See
.B USAGE.md
for comprehensive tutorials and additional examples.
.\"
.\" ===== DIAGNOSTICS =====
.\"
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B xf
reports errors and status messages with the following prefixes:
.TP
.B xf:
Informational message (file loaded, selection made, changes staged, etc.)
.TP
.B xf: error:
An error occurred (file not found, invalid command, etc.)
.PP
Common diagnostic messages:
.TP
.B "xf: no selection"
A transformation command was issued without first creating a selection.
Use a
.B select
command before transformation commands.
.TP
.B "xf: no staged changes"
The
.B preview
or
.B commit
command was issued when no changes are staged.
Use a transformation command (replace, delete, insert, move) first.
.TP
.B "xf: invalid regex pattern"
The regular expression in a
.B /regex/
pattern has a syntax error. Check the regex syntax and try again.
.TP
.B "xf: 0 range(s), 0 line(s) selected"
The selection or refinement matched nothing. Try a different pattern or
start with a broader selection.
.\"
.\" ===== ENVIRONMENT =====
.\"
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B xf
does not currently use any environment variables.
.\"
.\" ===== FILES =====
.\"
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.qi_history
Not currently implemented. Future versions may save command history here.
.TP
.I *.xf
By convention, script files for
.B xf
use the
.B .xf
extension, but any text file can be used.
.\"
.\" ===== EXIT STATUS =====
.\"
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful execution.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (e.g., file not found, invalid arguments).
.\"
.\" ===== CAVEATS =====
.\"
.SH CAVEATS
.IP \(bu 2
.B xf
does not warn about uncommitted changes when quitting. Always use
.B preview
before
.B quit
to ensure you have committed all desired changes.
.IP \(bu 2
The
.B select functions
command uses heuristic detection for C-style functions and may not correctly
identify all function definitions in complex or non-standard code.
.IP \(bu 2
.B xf
loads entire files into memory. Very large files (exceeding available RAM)
may cause performance issues or failures.
.IP \(bu 2
Regular expressions use POSIX Extended Regular Expression syntax, which differs
slightly from Perl-compatible regular expressions (PCRE) used by many modern
tools.
.IP \(bu 2
Undo is currently not supported in the traditional sense. Use
.B abort
to discard staged changes, or reload the file to revert committed changes.
